\begin_layout Section*
2.
 Random Assignment?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Part A
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Question: 
\series default
Some researcher claims that the tests conducted in (1) support the assertion
 that the treatment was randomly assigned to students and teachers.
 Do you agree? Explain.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Important to note, we are restricting our attention to new STAR participants
 only in each of kindergarten, 1st grade, 2nd grade, and 3rd grade.
 Now, without conditioning on school of attendance, I do not believe that
 the treatment was randomly assigned to students and teachers, especially
 for students entering STAR in later grades.
 This is because we are able to reject, at the 5% level, the null hypothesis
 that there are not significant differences in the means of the relevant
 control variables of students (free lunch, white/asian, and age in 1985)
 by treatment status.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Part B
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In fact, the treatment was randomly assigned to students and teachers within
 schools.
 For each of the variables employed in (1), test the null hypothesis that,
 conditional on school of attendance, there are no significant differences
 across treatment status.
 Once again, given both F-statistics and p-values in your answer, each rounded
 to three decimal places.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The table below gives the F-statistics and p-values of each variable for
 an F-test of equality, conditional on school of attendance, of all three
 treatment groups.
\end_layout

