\series default
Would you say that there is strong evidence of selection on observables?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In question 2, I determined that the treatment, in general, was not randomly
 assigned to students and teachers.
 However, the treatment was randomly assigned conditional on school of attendanc
e.
 Now, except for first grade, the coefficient on the small class indicator
 increases when I control for school fixed effects.
 This is evidence that the estimates in specification (1) are biased downwards
 because it is not associated with the true model.
 Specification (1) assumes that treatment was randomly assigned when, in
 fact, it was not.
 However, the coefficients still change quite a bit when I control for school
 fixed effects and I add control variables associated with student and teacher
 characteristics.
 This provides strong evidence of selection on observables.
\end_layout

