\begin_layout Standard
Construct a variable that gives each student's 
\emph on
initial
\emph default
 class assignment (i.e., initialassign).
 Then, construct an indicator for whether an individual was no longer in
 his initial class type (though is still participating in the program) in
 each later grade (i.e., classtrans).
 Krueger argues that transitions were nonrandom, though doesn't present
 any direct evidence on the characteristics of the switchers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Question: 
\series default
Do transitions appear to be random? Calculate any relevant or useful statistics
 in your answer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to determine if transitions appear to be random, I tested the null
 hypothesis that there is not a significant difference in the mean of the
 class transition indicator by initial class assignment.
 This is equivalent to saying that transitions were random.
 The alternative hypothesis, on the other hand, is that transitions were
 nonrandom.
 After formulating anova table, I obtained an F-statistic of 272.98 and a
 p-value that is essentially zero.
 So, we can reject the null hypothesis in favor of the alternative hypothesis
 at all standard significance levels.
 Therefore, as Krueger argued, transitions were nonrandom.
\end_layout

