\begin_layout Subsection*
Part D
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Question: 
\series default
What is the problem with this IV approach when considering the effects for
 non-kindergarteners?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The major problem with this IV approach when considering the effects for
 non-kindergarteners is that the IV does not satisfy the exclusion restriction.
 Since initial class assignment was random conditional on school of attendance,
 then I know the IV has no direct effect on the average percentile score.
 However, the IV can still influence the average percentile score through
 means other than treatment status.
 Even though I am not able to directly test if IV satisfies the exclusion
 restriction, I can still develop a legitimate story that enables me to
 conclude that it does not.
 To illustrate, it is not uncommon for students to repeat Kindergarten based
 on factors other than test performance, such as undeveloped social skills.
 In his paper, Krueger fails to consider this possibility.
 If students must repeat kindergarten, then they will be put into a new
 class with a different teacher, so their initial class assignment may change
 as a result.
 Therefore, the IV could affect average percentile score through means other
 than treatment status.
\end_layout

